{"parentUuid":"94a8d2f8-36f0-4c0d-b48c-18342c42decb","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VXxBzxzufZFqW68ndKoe3M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`ireturn` is a golangci-lint linter that enforces the Go proverb: **\"Accept interfaces, return concrete types.\"**\n\nIt flags functions whose return type is an interface rather than a concrete type. The rationale:\n\n- **Returning concrete types** gives callers more flexibility — they can use the full type or assign it to any interface it satisfies.\n- **Returning interfaces** hides the concrete type, limiting what callers can do and making the API less discoverable.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```go\n// ireturn flags this — returns an interface\nfunc NewStore() Store { return &fileStore{} }\n\n// ireturn is happy with this — returns concrete type\nfunc NewStore() *fileStore { return &fileStore{} }\n```\n\n**When `//nolint:ireturn` is appropriate:**\n\n- Factory functions that intentionally hide the implementation (e.g., strategy pattern, dependency injection)\n- Functions that genuinely need to return different concrete types depending on input\n- Interface-based APIs where the concrete type is unexported by design\n\nIn this codebase, you'd likely see it on functions like strategy constructors that return the `Strategy` interface, since the whole point is to abstract over multiple implementations."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31363,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":405,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31363,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CZUEhVhgwaaAKhoRHZjty","type":"assistant","uuid":"500b6658-d4a0-4674-ae44-8de27c730515","timestamp":"2026-03-27T20:44:31.619Z","userType":"external","entrypoint"